1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,360
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, where each week

2
00:00:03,413 --> 00:00:06,001
we explore the Torah portion through the eyes

3
00:00:06,054 --> 00:00:10,081
of Rashi, the great medieval commentator whose insights

4
00:00:10,113 --> 00:00:13,361
have illuminated Jewish learning for nearly a thousand

5
00:00:13,441 --> 00:00:14,161
years.

6
00:00:14,353 --> 00:00:18,481
This week's portion is Ha'Azinu, found in Deuteronomy

7
00:00:18,531 --> 00:00:21,201
chapter 32, and it contains one of the

8
00:00:21,265 --> 00:00:24,401
most powerful and poetic passages in the entire

9
00:00:24,441 --> 00:00:25,202
Torah.

10
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:30,830
Let's see what Rashi helps us discover.Ha'Azinu is

11
00:00:30,883 --> 00:00:33,150
essentially Moses's farewell song.

12
00:00:33,274 --> 00:00:36,110
Standing at the edge of the Promised Land,

13
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,230
knowing he will not cross over, Moses delivers

14
00:00:39,310 --> 00:00:42,270
a sweeping poem that calls heaven and earth

15
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:47,070
as witnesses, praises God's justice and faithfulness, recounts

16
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:49,710
how God found and nurtured Israel in the

17
00:00:49,761 --> 00:00:53,070
wilderness, and then warns of the consequences of

18
00:00:53,128 --> 00:00:54,750
forgetting that relationship.

19
00:00:54,990 --> 00:00:58,590
The song traces a dramatic arc — from

20
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:01,630
God's tender care for His people, compared to

21
00:01:01,657 --> 00:01:04,510
an eagle caring for its young, to Israel's

22
00:01:04,581 --> 00:01:09,950
eventual prosperity, complacency, and descent into idolatry, and

23
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:13,390
finally to the devastating consequences that follow.

24
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:17,150
Because this portion is written as poetry rather

25
00:01:17,166 --> 00:01:20,590
than narrative, Rashi faces a distinctive set of

26
00:01:20,641 --> 00:01:21,550
challenges here.

27
00:01:21,817 --> 00:01:26,590
He's working with dense metaphors, unusual vocabulary, and

28
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:28,590
imagery that needs unpacking.

29
00:01:28,782 --> 00:01:32,110
Many of his comments focus on clarifying what

30
00:01:32,163 --> 00:01:35,630
these poetic images actually mean, connecting them to

31
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:39,070
specific moments in Jewish history, and revealing the

32
00:01:39,134 --> 00:01:42,510
emotional and theological layers beneath the surface of

33
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:47,009
the language.Let's walk through some of Rashi's most

34
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:49,249
striking comments on this portion.

35
00:01:49,469 --> 00:01:52,929
The very first verse says, "Give ear, O

36
00:01:52,999 --> 00:01:55,489
heavens, and I will speak; and let the

37
00:01:55,569 --> 00:01:59,009
earth hear the words of my mouth." Rashi

38
00:01:59,073 --> 00:02:01,409
asks a question that might not immediately occur

39
00:02:01,462 --> 00:02:04,369
to us: why does Moses call on heaven

40
00:02:04,429 --> 00:02:06,449
and earth as his witnesses?

41
00:02:06,609 --> 00:02:10,529
His answer is deeply practical and deeply poignant.

42
00:02:10,676 --> 00:02:13,809
Moses says to himself, "I am flesh and

43
00:02:13,876 --> 00:02:14,609
blood.

44
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:16,449
Tomorrow I will be dead.

45
00:02:16,556 --> 00:02:19,249
If the Israelites later claim they never accepted

46
00:02:19,269 --> 00:02:21,809
the covenant, who will be there to contradict

47
00:02:21,857 --> 00:02:26,849
them?" Heaven and earth, unlike Moses, endure forever

48
00:02:26,929 --> 00:02:28,929
— they are eternal witnesses.

49
00:02:29,189 --> 00:02:32,689
But Rashi adds a second, beautiful layer.

50
00:02:32,796 --> 00:02:35,970
These witnesses aren't just passive observers.

51
00:02:36,130 --> 00:02:39,810
If Israel acts worthily, the witnesses themselves deliver

52
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:42,690
the reward — the heavens give rain, the

53
00:02:42,757 --> 00:02:44,690
earth yields its produce.

54
00:02:44,850 --> 00:02:47,890
And if Israel sins, the witnesses carry out

55
00:02:47,910 --> 00:02:51,010
the punishment — the heavens withhold rain, the

56
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:53,010
earth withholds its bounty.

57
00:02:53,277 --> 00:02:56,290
In other words, the natural world itself is

58
00:02:56,330 --> 00:02:58,370
the mechanism of consequence.

59
00:02:58,450 --> 00:03:02,757
The witnesses are also the enforcers.A few verses

60
00:03:02,837 --> 00:03:05,237
later, we encounter a line that has become

61
00:03:05,305 --> 00:03:09,797
foundational in Jewish theology: "The Rock, His work

62
00:03:09,904 --> 00:03:13,397
is perfect, a God of faithfulness and without

63
00:03:13,477 --> 00:03:19,317
injustice, righteous and upright is He." Rashi draws

64
00:03:19,397 --> 00:03:21,597
out a remarkable idea here.

65
00:03:21,744 --> 00:03:24,917
On the phrase "a God of faithfulness," he

66
00:03:24,979 --> 00:03:27,477
explains that God is faithful to reward the

67
00:03:27,549 --> 00:03:30,117
righteous in the world to come — even

68
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:33,077
though that reward may be delayed, God will

69
00:03:33,142 --> 00:03:35,237
ultimately keep His promise.

70
00:03:35,477 --> 00:03:40,037
Then on the words "and without injustice," Rashi

71
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:41,717
makes a surprising move.

72
00:03:41,957 --> 00:03:45,557
He says this means God is without injustice

73
00:03:45,685 --> 00:03:47,477
even toward the wicked.

74
00:03:47,737 --> 00:03:50,677
God pays even the wicked for their good

75
00:03:50,784 --> 00:03:52,837
deeds — but He pays them in this

76
00:03:52,904 --> 00:03:54,997
world, not the next.

77
00:03:55,344 --> 00:03:59,237
So divine justice is precise and complete in

78
00:03:59,333 --> 00:04:00,597
both directions.

79
00:04:00,917 --> 00:04:04,997
No good deed goes uncompensated, and no person

80
00:04:05,050 --> 00:04:08,597
is treated unfairly, whether righteous or wicked.

81
00:04:08,817 --> 00:04:13,546
The system is meticulous.One of the most tender

82
00:04:13,615 --> 00:04:16,109
images in the entire Torah appears in verse

83
00:04:16,178 --> 00:04:19,789
eleven: "As an eagle stirs up its nest,

84
00:04:19,829 --> 00:04:23,469
hovers over its young, spreads its wings, takes

85
00:04:23,517 --> 00:04:27,789
them, bears them on its pinions." Rashi unpacks

86
00:04:27,821 --> 00:04:30,029
this metaphor with extraordinary care.

87
00:04:30,296 --> 00:04:33,869
He explains that the eagle, unlike other birds,

88
00:04:33,901 --> 00:04:36,349
does not enter its nest suddenly.

89
00:04:36,419 --> 00:04:39,309
Instead, it beats its wings above the nest,

90
00:04:39,355 --> 00:04:42,189
moving between branches, so that the young birds

91
00:04:42,258 --> 00:04:44,749
awaken gently and have time to gather their

92
00:04:44,820 --> 00:04:45,469
strength.

93
00:04:45,689 --> 00:04:48,109
And when the eagle needs to carry its

94
00:04:48,176 --> 00:04:51,069
young from place to place, it doesn't grasp

95
00:04:51,117 --> 00:04:53,309
them in its talons the way other birds

96
00:04:53,389 --> 00:04:53,709
do.

97
00:04:53,969 --> 00:04:55,229
Why?

98
00:04:55,289 --> 00:04:58,029
Because other birds fear the eagle above them

99
00:04:58,109 --> 00:05:00,189
and carry their young in their claws to

100
00:05:00,249 --> 00:05:02,429
protect them from attack from above.

101
00:05:02,649 --> 00:05:06,029
But the eagle has no predator above —

102
00:05:06,109 --> 00:05:08,509
it fears only the arrow from below.

103
00:05:08,776 --> 00:05:11,549
So it carries its young on its wings,

104
00:05:11,618 --> 00:05:15,469
saying, in Rashi's vivid paraphrase, "Better that the

105
00:05:15,536 --> 00:05:18,029
arrow pierce me than that it pierce my

106
00:05:18,091 --> 00:05:22,429
children." This, Rashi says, is how God carried

107
00:05:22,532 --> 00:05:25,789
Israel — shielding them with His own presence,

108
00:05:25,859 --> 00:05:29,389
placing Himself between Israel and danger, just as

109
00:05:29,429 --> 00:05:31,789
the angel of God moved between the Egyptian

110
00:05:31,869 --> 00:05:33,869
camp and the Israelite camp at the Red

111
00:05:33,949 --> 00:05:38,409
Sea to absorb the arrows and stones.Later, after

112
00:05:38,467 --> 00:05:41,529
describing God's abundant blessings — honey from the

113
00:05:41,609 --> 00:05:44,809
rock, oil from the flinty stone, the finest

114
00:05:44,876 --> 00:05:47,449
wheat and wine — the poem takes a

115
00:05:47,516 --> 00:05:48,809
devastating turn.

116
00:05:48,929 --> 00:05:53,289
Verse fifteen says, "Jeshurun grew fat and kicked

117
00:05:53,529 --> 00:05:56,649
— you grew fat, you grew thick, you

118
00:05:56,706 --> 00:05:59,609
became gross — and he forsook the God

119
00:05:59,649 --> 00:06:02,809
who made him." Rashi zeroes in on the

120
00:06:02,889 --> 00:06:06,489
word "kashita," which he says means "you became

121
00:06:06,559 --> 00:06:08,969
covered" — like a person so full of

122
00:06:09,022 --> 00:06:12,089
internal fat that his flesh lies in folds

123
00:06:12,142 --> 00:06:13,129
on the outside.

124
00:06:13,289 --> 00:06:16,089
The image is visceral and unflattering.

125
00:06:16,209 --> 00:06:20,009
Prosperity didn't just make Israel comfortable; it made

126
00:06:20,057 --> 00:06:23,929
them unrecognizable, encased in layers of excess that

127
00:06:24,049 --> 00:06:25,529
hid their true selves.

128
00:06:25,709 --> 00:06:29,289
And the very next phrase, "he lightly esteemed

129
00:06:29,329 --> 00:06:32,649
the Rock of his salvation," Rashi illustrates with

130
00:06:32,689 --> 00:06:36,089
a shocking image from Ezekiel: people standing in

131
00:06:36,109 --> 00:06:38,889
the Temple with their backs turned toward God's

132
00:06:38,945 --> 00:06:39,529
sanctuary.

133
00:06:39,649 --> 00:06:42,889
There can be no greater contempt, Rashi says,

134
00:06:42,953 --> 00:06:46,489
than this — not active rebellion, but casual

135
00:06:46,561 --> 00:06:49,609
dismissal, turning one's back on the source of

136
00:06:49,674 --> 00:06:53,985
everything.Finally, when God describes the punishment He will

137
00:06:54,038 --> 00:06:57,185
bring, He says in verse twenty-three, "I will

138
00:06:57,265 --> 00:07:00,225
heap evils upon them; I will use up

139
00:07:00,358 --> 00:07:04,785
My arrows against them." Rashi offers two readings

140
00:07:04,838 --> 00:07:07,185
of this verse, and the second one contains

141
00:07:07,265 --> 00:07:08,465
a hidden blessing.

142
00:07:08,678 --> 00:07:11,985
He reads it this way: "My arrows will

143
00:07:12,033 --> 00:07:14,865
come to an end, but they themselves will

144
00:07:14,965 --> 00:07:18,305
not come to an end." In other words,

145
00:07:18,367 --> 00:07:21,505
embedded within the very language of punishment is

146
00:07:21,545 --> 00:07:23,265
a promise of survival.

147
00:07:23,505 --> 00:07:26,945
God's arsenal of suffering is finite, but the

148
00:07:27,002 --> 00:07:28,705
Jewish people are not.

149
00:07:28,945 --> 00:07:32,225
Even in the darkest moment of the poem,

150
00:07:32,278 --> 00:07:35,025
Rashi finds an assurance of endurance.

151
00:07:35,185 --> 00:07:38,545
The curse, by its very wording, implies that

152
00:07:38,625 --> 00:07:44,487
Israel will outlast every catastrophe.Stepping back, we can

153
00:07:44,547 --> 00:07:48,487
see several powerful themes running through Rashi's commentary

154
00:07:48,567 --> 00:07:49,927
on Ha'Azinu.

155
00:07:50,007 --> 00:07:53,127
One is the precision of divine justice —

156
00:07:53,187 --> 00:07:57,647
God's system is not arbitrary but perfectly calibrated,

157
00:07:57,699 --> 00:08:01,527
rewarding and punishing with exacting fairness, even across

158
00:08:01,547 --> 00:08:04,487
the boundary between this world and the next.

159
00:08:04,597 --> 00:08:08,487
Another is the danger of prosperity: Rashi shows

160
00:08:08,567 --> 00:08:11,367
us again and again how abundance leads to

161
00:08:11,434 --> 00:08:15,527
complacency, how fullness leads to forgetfulness.

162
00:08:15,667 --> 00:08:18,807
But perhaps the deepest theme is God's tenderness

163
00:08:18,903 --> 00:08:20,687
even within severity.

164
00:08:20,737 --> 00:08:22,887
The eagle who shields its young with its

165
00:08:22,947 --> 00:08:25,687
own body, the arrows that run out before

166
00:08:25,707 --> 00:08:27,847
the people do — these are images of

167
00:08:27,887 --> 00:08:31,367
a God who punishes but never abandons.

168
00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:34,487
Rashi reads this poem not as a prediction

169
00:08:34,514 --> 00:08:37,127
of doom, but as what he calls "words

170
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:40,007
of reproof" — a parent's warning born of

171
00:08:40,087 --> 00:08:42,487
love, meant to call a people back to

172
00:08:42,567 --> 00:08:47,395
awareness before it's too late.Thank you so much

173
00:08:47,415 --> 00:08:49,395
for joining me on Rashi in Brief this

174
00:08:49,459 --> 00:08:49,875
week.

175
00:08:50,035 --> 00:08:53,395
Wishing you a meaningful week of learning, and

176
00:08:53,515 --> 00:08:54,835
I'll see you next time.

